00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.280
FELIPE HOFFA: And this one
jumped all the way back

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.921
to 20 servers at the beginning?

00:07:48.921 --> 00:07:50.920
GRAHAM POLLEY: Zero, 20,
and then went back down

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.070
to 10, and back up to 20
again, and then, eventually,

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:54.060
the whole cluster was torn up.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.184
FELIPE HOFFA: That's great.

00:07:55.184 --> 00:07:57.132
So less servers are
needed in some steps--

00:07:57.132 --> 00:07:58.090
GRAHAM POLLEY: Correct.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:59.690
FELIPE HOFFA: --as
autoscaling goes down.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.773
GRAHAM POLLEY: But we don't
determine those steps.

00:08:01.773 --> 00:08:04.079
Dataflow does all that
for us with autoscaling.

00:08:04.079 --> 00:08:04.870
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.332
GRAHAM POLLEY: So even
if we go back here,

00:08:07.332 --> 00:08:11.405
we can see all the pipelines
that have run in production.

00:08:11.405 --> 00:08:13.530
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, so these
are running permanently,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:15.980
like many times a day, all
thanks to Cloud Functions?

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.010
GRAHAM POLLEY:
Yeah, all triggering

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:17.950
from Cloud Functions.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.110
FELIPE HOFFA: And then
you have the templates.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:19.916
GRAHAM POLLEY: That's right.

00:08:19.916 --> 00:08:20.332
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:08:20.332 --> 00:08:22.165
GRAHAM POLLEY: How close
are we to the city?

00:08:22.165 --> 00:08:23.385
We'd better do a check--

00:08:23.385 --> 00:08:25.142
oh, we're at Fed Square, OK.

00:08:25.142 --> 00:08:29.158
So we'd better
check our pipeline.

00:08:29.158 --> 00:08:32.480
So how are we doing?

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:33.630
OK.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:34.475
Almost there.

00:08:34.475 --> 00:08:35.600
FELIPE HOFFA: Almost there?

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:36.285
Running--

00:08:36.285 --> 00:08:38.809
GRAHAM POLLEY: We're up
to 9 billion records now.

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:39.809
FELIPE HOFFA: Excellent.

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:42.860
GRAHAM POLLEY: 567 gig.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.705
FELIPE HOFFA: Excellent.

00:08:44.705 --> 00:08:47.440
GRAHAM POLLEY: So we've got
to just wait one more minute,

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.784
I would say, and then
we'll hit the city,

00:08:49.784 --> 00:08:51.950
and hopefully the pipeline
will be finished by then.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.533
FELIPE HOFFA: And
hopefully-- perfect.

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:56.245
Any other tips that
you have for people

00:08:56.245 --> 00:08:57.800
doing stuff with Dataflow?

00:09:00.266 --> 00:09:02.140
GRAHAM POLLEY: The great
thing about Dataflow

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.680
is it allows you to
concentrate on the code itself,

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.800
rather than worry about
any of the infrastructure.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.980
The beauty of
Dataflow, as well, it

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.564
runs at both batch
and streaming mode.

00:09:11.564 --> 00:09:12.313
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:13.895
GRAHAM POLLEY: So you
write once, but run it

00:09:13.895 --> 00:09:14.700
in batch and streaming.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:15.630
It's really helpful.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.420
So if you've got
streaming realtime data,

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.050
you can also use
Dataflow, which is great.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.841
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, we
did this with batch,

00:09:20.841 --> 00:09:22.109
but you're also using it for--

00:09:22.109 --> 00:09:23.900
GRAHAM POLLEY: For
streaming as well, yeah.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.142
And we do a lot of BigQuery
streaming on top of that,

00:09:26.142 --> 00:09:29.700
and we mix the tools together
to solve the problems

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:30.730
that we need to solve.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:31.521
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:09:31.521 --> 00:09:33.390
And you also need
to decide sometimes,

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.320
do you use BigQuery or
do you use Dataflow?

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:36.030
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah,
so it's picking

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.113
the best tool for the job.

00:09:37.113 --> 00:09:37.840
Right?

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.890
So BigQuery's great at smashing
true data and aggregating it,

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.450
and Dataflow is really good
for pulling all that data in,

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.910
transforming it, and
sending it to BigQuery

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:45.840
for further analysis.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.620
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice, and
how are we doing here?

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:48.020
GRAHAM POLLEY: We're done.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:48.860
FELIPE HOFFA: We're done.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:49.410
Excellent.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.267
Time for a pint.

00:09:50.267 --> 00:09:51.350
GRAHAM POLLEY: Definitely.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:52.740
FELIPE HOFFA: Awesome.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:56.270
So we made it back to the
Shine offices on time.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:57.180
Challenge completed.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.240
GRAHAM POLLEY:
Challenge completed.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.500
FELIPE HOFFA: That
was pretty nice.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.510
GRAHAM POLLEY: That
was a great challenge.

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:01.260
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:02.850
How much data you process, then?

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.490
GRAHAM POLLEY: We processed
over 10 billion records that

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:10.190
were in GCS, about 500
gigabytes, almost half

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.470
a terabyte, all in under 10
minutes using Cloud Dataflow.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.720
FELIPE HOFFA: In under 10
minutes, 10 billion records?

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:16.710
That's like--

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:19.960
GRAHAM POLLEY: 29
million records a second

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.727
being read and
transformed in parallel.

00:10:21.727 --> 00:10:23.310
FELIPE HOFFA: While
we were tethering?

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.769
GRAHAM POLLEY: While we were
tethering off my mobile phone.

00:10:25.769 --> 00:10:27.143
FELIPE HOFFA:
That's pretty cool.

00:10:27.143 --> 00:10:28.770
Who handled all the complexity?

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:29.430
GRAHAM POLLEY: Well,
the beauty of it

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.600
is Dataflow handled all the
complexity of it, right?

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.100
So it spun up the
worker pool, autoscaled

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.590
VMs, we went up to 50 VMs,
and then when it was finished,

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:38.922
tore the whole cluster down.

00:10:38.922 --> 00:10:40.380
FELIPE HOFFA: That
was pretty nice.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:40.920
GRAHAM POLLEY: Very nice.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:41.670
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:42.870
So what do we do now?

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:44.660
GRAHAM POLLEY: That's
a tough question.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:45.377
Drink a beer?

00:10:45.377 --> 00:10:46.085
FELIPE HOFFA: OK.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:49.895
Salud.

00:10:49.895 --> 00:10:50.770
GRAHAM POLLEY: Salud.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.320
Prost.

